<GuideArticlePage
  title="How Often to Service a Rolex? | Shelton Watch Services"
  description="Rolex service intervals explained — official guidance vs. independent recommendations for daily wear, plus what changes the timeline."
  h1="How Often Should You Service a Rolex Watch?"
  answer="Rolex has publicly cited up to about 10 years between services for modern movements under normal use, while many independents still plan on roughly 5–7 years for hard daily wear — especially on bracelets that see water, gym bags, and temperature swings. The honest answer blends manufacturer guidance with how you actually use the watch."
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHref="/service/rolex/"
  ctaLabel="Book Rolex service →"
>
  <p>
    Intervals are not morality — they are risk management. A Datejust that sees desk duty and occasional
    weekends behaves differently than a Submariner that lives in salt air. Roughly <strong>60–70%</strong>{' '}
    of the “should I wait?” conversations we have in the Lehigh Valley come down to environment and
    shock history, not calendar age alone.
  </p>
  <h2>What Does Rolex Officially Recommend?</h2>
  <p>
    Rolex’s modern positioning emphasizes long intervals for recently manufactured watches, reflecting
    improvements in oils, alloys, and manufacturing consistency. That guidance assumes “normal” wear — not
    ignored water exposure, not magnetism from laptops, and not years of ignored amplitude loss.
  </p>
  <h2>When Do Independents Suggest Service Sooner Than a Decade?</h2>
  <p>
    Many working watchmakers still use a <strong>5–7 year</strong> mental model for automatics that run
    daily — not because they want work, but because lubricants age and wear accelerates once friction
    rises. If your watch suddenly gains or loses time in multiple positions, that is data — not a fluke.
  </p>
  <h2>What Signals Mean You Should Move Service Up?</h2>
  <p>
    Crown grit, date snap hesitation, poor power reserve versus factory spec, and obvious magnetism after
    travel are all reasons to stop waiting. A timing machine trace can show problems while the watch still
    “looks fine” on the wrist — which is why evaluations beat guessing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Still pricing the decision? Read <a href="/guide/rolex-service-cost/">how much Rolex service costs</a>{' '}
    — then talk with a bench that explains the estimate line by line. If you are weighing an exit instead,
    compare notes on <a href="/sell/rolex/">selling your Rolex</a>.
  </p>
</GuideArticlePage>
